# name: test/sql/macro_from_parity_source_of.test
# description: M2 stage 3 -- ast_source_of_from agrees with its path-taking twin, and carries a files argument
# group: [sitting_duck]
#
# WHAT THIS GUARDS
# ----------------
# ast_source_of gained a `_from` variant that reads an already-parsed AST table
# with query_table() instead of calling read_ast() itself. The twin was then
# rewritten as a thin wrapper that parses and delegates, following ast_select /
# ast_select_from, task 4's five relational operators and task 5's three
# navigation accessors.
#
# THIS ONE KEEPS A SECOND ARGUMENT, AND HAS TO. ast_source_of returns the SOURCE
# TEXT of each match, which it gets by joining read_text(file_patterns)
# (src/sql_macros/file_utilities.sql, the `file_contents` CTE). A node table
# carries no source text, so the paths have to come along:
#
#   ast_source_of_from(ast_table, files, target_name, kind := NULL)
#
# exactly as ast_unparse_exact_from(ast_table, files, ...) already does. The
# alternative -- dropping the `source` column -- would leave the two twins with
# different column lists, and the EXCEPT ALL pairs below need the same six
# columns on both sides to bind at all.
#
# THE EXCEPT ALL PAIRS BELOW COMPARED TWO INDEPENDENT BODIES ONCE: on the build
# where ast_source_of_from existed and the twin did NOT yet delegate to it. From
# the wrapper rewrite onwards both sides evaluate the same body, so a defect
# INSIDE the `_from` body moves both sides at once and the pairs cannot see it.
# What they do still see is a defect in the WRAPPER, which is the only part that
# differs between the two sides: an argument forwarded to the wrong parameter
# (and this macro has two positional arguments after the table, so that risk is
# higher here than in tasks 4 and 5), a changed column list, or a row duplicated
# or dropped.
#
# EXCEPT ALL, not EXCEPT. Set semantics cannot see multiplicity at all --
# MEASURED on a planted build in task 5: a row duplication read 148 through
# EXCEPT ALL while plain EXCEPT read 0. A wrapper that doubled every row would
# pass a plain EXCEPT in both directions.
#
# THE PINNED COUNTS AND DIGESTS ARE WHAT CATCH A DEFECT INSIDE THE `_from` BODY.
# Where each pin came from, stated exactly, because "measured" and "inferred"
# are not the same claim:
#
#   EVERY pin in sections 1 to 5 was MEASURED on commit 2ba392f, BEFORE this
#   task's changes. The ones a twin can produce (sections 1, 2 and 3) were
#   measured from the twin itself. The ones that pass a TABLE, which no twin can
#   take, were measured on that same pre-change build from a hand-written
#   reconstruction of the proposed `_from` body (the twin's body with read_ast
#   replaced by the table) -- an independent oracle, but not the twin. The
#   reconstruction agreed with the twin on all four EXCEPT ALL directions and on
#   every count and digest.
#
#   SECTION 6 has NO pre-change measurement: a missing or empty table is a
#   `_from`-only input. It follows the convention task 4's section 8 and task
#   5's section 9 established, and was measured on the build that first had the
#   real macro.
#
# EVERY DIGEST HERE COVERS THE WHOLE ROW:
#
#   md5(string_agg(r::VARCHAR, ',' ORDER BY r.file_path || ':' || r.start_line))
#
# Task 4's review measured why. A count plus a key digest cannot see a COLUMN
# CHANGE: planting `SELECT a.* EXCLUDE (peek)` in ast_has_from left the row count
# and every key untouched, and 51 of 52 assertions green. Casting the row alias
# to VARCHAR prints every column NAME and value, so a dropped, added or renamed
# column moves the digest. It does NOT see every retype: MEASURED,
# `{'x': 5::INTEGER}::VARCHAR = {'x': 5::BIGINT}::VARCHAR` is true, because both
# render `{'x': 5}`. A widened or narrowed numeric type is therefore invisible
# here; a retype that changes the rendered text is not. (to_json() would do the
# same, but it needs the json extension, which this harness does not load -- and
# a `require json` that went unmet would skip this whole file silently, turning
# a failure into a vacuous pass.) The cast's exact text is DuckDB's struct
# representation, so a change to THAT also moves these digests; re-measure
# against the pre-change body rather than editing them. A pin that moves is a
# parity failure to investigate, never a value to update.
#
# THE ORDER KEY IS file_path || ':' || start_line, AND NOT file_path || ':' ||
# name. Every row this macro returns carries the SAME name, because the
# `matches` CTE filters on `name = target_name`
# (src/sql_macros/file_utilities.sql). Ordering on name is ordering on a
# constant: it would degrade the digest to per-file order and lose all power
# inside a file that holds two definitions of the target name -- which this
# fixture has. (file_path, start_line) is the macro's own documented order, from
# its closing `ORDER BY file_path, start_line`. Section 1 MEASURES that the key
# is unique in this result rather than assuming it: a digest built on a
# non-unique key is order-dependent between runs and flakes.
#
# THE FIXTURE IS test/data/python/q*.py, WHICH MATCHES EXACTLY TWO FILES, AND
# THE TARGET IS `__init__`. Measured, pre-change: 4 matches over both files,
# THREE of them in qualified_names.py at lines 5, 13 and 17. So the result spans
# two files (a body that collapsed to one file_path, dropped a file or crossed
# rows between files is visible) AND orders several rows inside one file (the
# within-file half of the ORDER BY is exercised).

require sitting_duck

# The pre-parsed table every `_from` call below reads. Default extraction
# options, exactly as the twin's own read_ast() call uses them, or the EXCEPT
# would differ in a column rather than in the lookup.
statement ok
CREATE TABLE tq AS SELECT * FROM read_ast('test/data/python/q*.py');

# =============================================================================
# 1. Preconditions: the baseline is not empty, and the digest key is unique
# =============================================================================

# Pinned exactly, not as `> 0`: a `> 0` baseline survives a body that loses most
# of its rows. Measured pre-change from the twin.
query I
SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of('test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__');
----
4

# The two preconditions the digests below depend on, asserted as properties
# rather than as the measured 4 and 2, so a fixture change does not fail these
# lines for the wrong reason:
#   - the result spans BOTH files, or a dropped file could not show up;
#   - (file_path, start_line) is unique per row, or `ORDER BY` on it is not a
#     total order and the digest is non-deterministic.
# MEASURED pre-change: 4 rows, 4 distinct keys, 2 distinct file_paths.
query II
SELECT count(DISTINCT r.file_path), count(*) = count(DISTINCT r.file_path || ':' || r.start_line)
FROM ast_source_of('test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__') r;
----
2	true

# The within-file half of the order key earns its keep: one file holds three of
# the four matches, so ordering by file_path alone would not be a total order
# either. Asserted as a property; measured pre-change as 3 in
# qualified_names.py.
query I
SELECT max(n) > 1 FROM (
  SELECT count(*) AS n FROM ast_source_of('test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__') GROUP BY file_path
);
----
true

# =============================================================================
# 2. Parity: both directions, then the whole row
# =============================================================================
#
# `files` is the SAME glob the twin is given. The two arguments have to agree
# about which files are in play; section 5 pins what happens when they do not.

query I
SELECT count(*) FROM (
  SELECT * FROM ast_source_of('test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__')
  EXCEPT ALL SELECT * FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', 'test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__')
);
----
0

# The other direction, which catches a `_from` form returning extra rows.
query I
SELECT count(*) FROM (
  SELECT * FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', 'test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__')
  EXCEPT ALL SELECT * FROM ast_source_of('test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__')
);
----
0

# The whole row, asserted twice -- once through the `_from` form, once through
# the glob-taking twin -- and both carry the SAME pre-change measurement, so the
# wrapper must not change a single column on the way out.
query II
SELECT count(*), md5(string_agg(r::VARCHAR, ',' ORDER BY r.file_path || ':' || r.start_line))
FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', 'test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__') r;
----
4	36873b0668f9e9bad57a38222c004006

query II
SELECT count(*), md5(string_agg(r::VARCHAR, ',' ORDER BY r.file_path || ':' || r.start_line))
FROM ast_source_of('test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__') r;
----
4	36873b0668f9e9bad57a38222c004006

# =============================================================================
# 3. The `kind` argument, which section 2 leaves NULL
# =============================================================================
#
# Section 2 cannot see a wrapper that drops or misroutes `kind`, because NULL is
# also what a lost named argument looks like. Each pair below is asserted twice,
# once per side, against the same pre-change measurement.
#
# TWO TARGETS, BECAUSE ONE WILL NOT DO. `__init__` is a function four times
# over, so `kind := 'class'` on it returns 0 -- which proves `kind` is applied
# but not that it admits anything. The empty name is the one target in this
# fixture that carries TWO kinds, so it splits non-empty into non-empty, and
# that is the half a zero cannot prove. Measured pre-change:
#
#   target      kind := NULL   kind := 'module'   kind := 'variable'
#   --------    ------------   ----------------   ------------------
#   ''                     7                  2                   5
#
#   target      kind := NULL   kind := 'function'   kind := 'class'
#   --------    ------------   ------------------   ---------------
#   '__init__'             4                    4                 0
#   'Account'              1                    0                 1
#
# The empty name is a real result, not a quirk of this test: unlike
# ast_definitions, ast_source_of does NOT filter `name != ''`, so the module
# node and the un-named variable targets of this fixture are reachable by it.
# That is pinned here as pre-existing behaviour, and is not something this task
# changed.

query IIII
SELECT
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', 'test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__', kind := 'function')),
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of('test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__', kind := 'function')),
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', 'test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__', kind := 'class')),
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of('test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__', kind := 'class'));
----
4	4	0	0

# The non-empty-into-non-empty split, and its own kind := NULL control beside
# it. Without the 7 the two filtered counts could both come from a body that
# lost rows for an unrelated reason.
query IIIIII
SELECT
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', 'test/data/python/q*.py', '')),
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of('test/data/python/q*.py', '')),
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', 'test/data/python/q*.py', '', kind := 'module')),
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of('test/data/python/q*.py', '', kind := 'module')),
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', 'test/data/python/q*.py', '', kind := 'variable')),
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of('test/data/python/q*.py', '', kind := 'variable'));
----
7	7	2	2	5	5

# The whole row for the seven-row result, so a column change on the kind := NULL
# path over a multi-kind target is also visible.
query II
SELECT count(*), md5(string_agg(r::VARCHAR, ',' ORDER BY r.file_path || ':' || r.start_line))
FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', 'test/data/python/q*.py', '') r;
----
7	c39db0870afaf395a4bac17ceb90e317

# `kind` admits as well as rejects on a single-row target, in both directions.
query IIII
SELECT
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', 'test/data/python/q*.py', 'Account', kind := 'class')),
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of('test/data/python/q*.py', 'Account', kind := 'class')),
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', 'test/data/python/q*.py', 'Account', kind := 'function')),
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of('test/data/python/q*.py', 'Account', kind := 'function'));
----
1	1	0	0

# =============================================================================
# 4. The `language` parameter, which only the twin has
# =============================================================================
#
# The twin keeps a `language` parameter the `_from` form does not have, and a
# wrapper that dropped it would silently fall back to extension detection --
# which on a .py file gives the right answer, so a row count cannot see the
# loss. A language the files do not match leaves read_ast with no file to read,
# and that error is visible. Measured pre-change.

statement error
SELECT * FROM ast_source_of('test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__', language := 'javascript');
----
read_ast needs at least one file to read

# =============================================================================
# 5. When `files` and `ast_table` disagree
# =============================================================================
#
# This hazard belongs to this macro alone: it is the only `_from` form in the
# stage that takes BOTH a table and a path, so the two can name different sets
# of files. The twin cannot reach this state, because it derives both from one
# argument.
#
# DECIDED BEHAVIOUR: A NARROWER `files` NARROWS THE RESULT, SILENTLY. The
# `file_contents` CTE inner-joins read_text(files) on the exact filename, so a
# match whose file is absent from `files` is dropped with no error. Narrowing
# `files` to one of the two fixture files therefore returns exactly the three
# matches in that file -- and MEASURED pre-change, those are the same three rows
# the twin returns for that single path, whole row for whole row. So this is
# pinned against the twin on the narrowed path, in both directions and by digest.
#
# This is not the loud-failure convention ast_unparse_exact_from follows (#89),
# which normalises path spellings and errors on an unreadable file. The
# difference is recorded in the macro's banner as a known limitation, not fixed
# here: the fix is a path-normalising join plus a readability guard, which is
# its own change with its own test.

query I
SELECT count(*) FROM (
  SELECT * FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', 'test/data/python/qualified_names.py', '__init__')
  EXCEPT ALL SELECT * FROM ast_source_of('test/data/python/qualified_names.py', '__init__')
);
----
0

query I
SELECT count(*) FROM (
  SELECT * FROM ast_source_of('test/data/python/qualified_names.py', '__init__')
  EXCEPT ALL SELECT * FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', 'test/data/python/qualified_names.py', '__init__')
);
----
0

# The count and the whole row. The 3 against section 2's 4 is what proves
# `files` is read at all and not ignored in favour of the table's own paths.
query II
SELECT count(*), md5(string_agg(r::VARCHAR, ',' ORDER BY r.file_path || ':' || r.start_line))
FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', 'test/data/python/qualified_names.py', '__init__') r;
----
3	04b6db7f1a4c16d937b1287559809f23

# And the file that `files` excluded is the one that is gone, rather than three
# arbitrary rows surviving.
query I
SELECT count(DISTINCT file_path) FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', 'test/data/python/qualified_names.py', '__init__');
----
1

# THE MIXED-SPELLING TRAP, which is the narrowing above turned harmful. The two
# arguments can name the SAME file in two spellings, and then the join misses
# everything. MEASURED on this build:
#
#   * a GLOB is normalised by both read_ast and read_text --
#     './test/data/python/q*.py' and 'test/data/python/q*.py' both yield
#     'test/data/python/...', so mixing those two spellings is harmless;
#   * an EXACT path is NOT normalised -- './test/data/python/qualified_names.py'
#     keeps its './' in both functions.
#
# So pairing a './'-prefixed exact path with an unprefixed glob returns 0 rows
# and raises nothing, in either direction. That is pinned here so it is defined
# behaviour rather than an accident, with the 4 and the 3 beside it as the
# positive controls: the same calls with agreeing spellings return rows, so a 0
# below is a statement about the spelling and not a broken query.
# ast_unparse_exact_from normalises for matching and errors instead (#89); see
# the KNOWN LIMITATION note on the macro.

statement ok
CREATE TABLE tqd AS SELECT * FROM read_ast('./test/data/python/qualified_names.py');

query IIIII
SELECT
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', './test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__')),
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', 'test/data/python/qualified_names.py', '__init__')),
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', './test/data/python/qualified_names.py', '__init__')),
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of_from('tqd', './test/data/python/qualified_names.py', '__init__')),
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of_from('tqd', 'test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__'));
----
4	3	0	3	0

# =============================================================================
# 6. A missing table names itself, a swapped pair names itself, and an empty
#    table is not an error
# =============================================================================
#
# These three inputs are `_from`-only, so no twin could have produced their
# values and they carry NO pre-change measurement. They were measured on the
# build that first had the real macro, and follow the convention task 4's
# section 8 and task 5's section 9 established.

# The expected text is the catalog phrase, not the bare table name. DuckDB
# appends a `LINE 1:` echo of the failing statement to this message, and that
# echo contains the string 'no_such_table' -- so an expectation of the name
# alone is also satisfied by any OTHER error on this statement that carries the
# echo. The phrase below names the table and can only come from the real error.
statement error
SELECT * FROM ast_source_of_from('no_such_table', 'test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__');
----
Table with name no_such_table does not exist

# THE ARGUMENT-ORDER GUARD, which this macro needs and the one-argument `_from`
# forms do not: its first two arguments are both strings, so exchanging them is
# a mistake the type system cannot catch. It fails loudly, because query_table()
# is handed a glob and no table carries that name. Measured.
statement error
SELECT * FROM ast_source_of_from('test/data/python/q*.py', 'tq', '__init__');
----
Table with name test/data/python/q*.py does not exist

# Well-formed and empty: the table has read_ast's columns and no rows. The
# `_from` macro must return 0 rows rather than raise. The 0 on the left is what
# makes the 0 on the right a statement about the macro: both come from the same
# query, so an empty input cannot be mistaken for an empty answer.
statement ok
CREATE TABLE empty_ast AS SELECT * FROM read_ast('test/data/python/q*.py') WHERE false;

query II
SELECT
  (SELECT count(*) FROM empty_ast),
  (SELECT count(*) FROM ast_source_of_from('empty_ast', 'test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__'));
----
0	0

# =============================================================================
# 7. The documented ordering, which no digest above can see
# =============================================================================
#
# ast_source_of ends in `ORDER BY file_path, start_line`, and that ordering is
# documented behaviour. Nothing above can see it: every digest imposes its own
# ORDER BY, and EXCEPT ALL is a set operation that ignores order entirely. After
# the wrapper rewrite the ORDER BY lives in the `_from` body, and the twin
# reaches it only through `SELECT * FROM ast_source_of_from(...)` -- which SQL
# does not oblige to preserve an inner ORDER BY. So it is pinned on both paths.
#
# list_sort is a total order, so this compares the EMITTED sequence against the
# sorted one rather than pinning a literal emission order.
#
# THIS CHECK IS NOT VACUOUS ON THIS FIXTURE, AND THAT HAD TO BE MEASURED.
# Task 5 found the opposite for ast_definitions: over a 2-file fixture its
# ordering boolean returned true even for a body with NO ORDER BY, because
# read_ast emits files in sorted order and DFS pre-order makes start_line
# non-decreasing -- so that check was a snapshot until the fixture was widened
# to 19 files. This macro does not inherit that, because the two joins
# (read_text, then file_contents) reshuffle the rows. MEASURED here, on a
# hand-written copy of the `_from` body with the ORDER BY STRIPPED and nothing
# else changed, three runs each:
#
#   fixture / target                 ordered   rows
#   ------------------------------   -------   ----
#   q*.py  / '__init__'              false        4
#   q*.py  / ''                      false        7
#   *.py   / '__init__'              false       12
#   *.py   / ''                      false       43
#
# false on EVERY fixture, including the two used below. So no widening is
# needed, and the exact-count pin of section 1 stays the fixture's guard.
#
# THE ROW TERM IS A FLOOR, and what it guards is an EMPTY result. MEASURED: over
# a target with no matches the boolean is NULL, not true, and the floor is
# false -- so neither term can pass on silence. The floor is stated as `>=`
# rather than `=` because section 1 already pins the count exactly; a second
# exact pin of the same number would only add a line to edit when the fixture
# changes.

query II
SELECT list(k) = list_sort(list(k)), count(*) >= 4
FROM (SELECT {'f': file_path, 's': start_line} AS k
      FROM ast_source_of('test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__'));
----
true	true

query II
SELECT list(k) = list_sort(list(k)), count(*) >= 4
FROM (SELECT {'f': file_path, 's': start_line} AS k
      FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', 'test/data/python/q*.py', '__init__'));
----
true	true

# The seven-row result as well, which spreads more rows across the same two
# files and so exercises the within-file half of the ordering harder.
query II
SELECT list(k) = list_sort(list(k)), count(*) >= 7
FROM (SELECT {'f': file_path, 's': start_line} AS k
      FROM ast_source_of_from('tq', 'test/data/python/q*.py', ''));
----
true	true
